# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The negation (!) matters: .env* would otherwise swallow .env.example, and the
# template is the one file here that MUST be committed.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Streamlit's own secrets mechanism. We don't use it (we use .env), but if you
# ever try st.secrets, this is where the keys would land.
.streamlit/secrets.toml

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Virtual environment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
env/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python build artefacts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test + tool caches
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-run token/cost logs (Phase 8) and the input-gate trigger log (Phase 7).
# These contain real user messages, so they are operational data, not source.
runs/
*.log

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editor / OS noise
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# .vscode/ is intentionally NOT ignored — committing shared editor settings
# (interpreter path, formatter) helps a second person get running. Uncomment
# the line below if you'd rather keep your local editor config private.
# .vscode/


# ===========================================================================
# Two things .gitignore does NOT do. Both have bitten real projects.
# ===========================================================================
#
# 1. It does not untrack a file that is ALREADY committed.
#    If .env is ever committed, adding it here changes nothing: git keeps
#    tracking it, and the value stays in history forever — recoverable by
#    anyone who clones the repo, even after you "delete" it in a later commit.
#    The only correct response to a committed key is to ROTATE THE KEY.
#    Deleting the file is cleanup; rotating is the fix.
#
# 2. It does not keep files out of the Docker image.
#    `docker build` reads .dockerignore, not .gitignore. A repo with a perfect
#    .gitignore can still bake .env into a published image via `COPY . .`,
#    where `docker history` will happily show it. We write .dockerignore in
#    Phase 11 for exactly this reason.
